REGENT OF SOUTH BANGKA

BANGKA BELITUNG ISLANDS PROVINCE

REGIONAL REGULATION OF SOUTH BANGKA REGENCY NUMBER 9 OF 2018

CONCERNING

WOMEN'S EMPOWERMENT AND PROTECTION BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

REGENT OF SOUTH BANGKA,

Whereas : a. that women and men have equal rights to participate in various fields of life;

b. that women are a national asset who play a crucial role in the process of succession and creation of a quality generation and therefore need to receive protection from violence and discrimination as well as need to be empowered to actualize their potential optimally;

c. that based on the considerations referred to in letter a, and letter b, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning Women's Empowerment and Protection;

In View Of : 1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;

2. Law Number 1 of 1974 concerning Marriage (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1974 Number 1, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3019);

3. Law Number 7 of 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on The Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1984 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3277);

4. Law Number 13 of 1998 concerning Elderly Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1998 Number 190, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3796);

5. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);

6. Law Number 27 of 2000 concerning Establishment of Bangka Belitung Islands Province (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2000 Number 217, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4033);

7. Law Number 5 of 2003 concerning Establishment of South Bangka Regency, Central Bangka Regency, West Bangka Regency and East Belitung Regency in Bangka Belitung Islands Province (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2003 Number 25, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4268);

8. Law Number 13 of 2003 concerning Manpower (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2003 Number 39, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4279);

9. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);

10. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);

11. Law Number 11 of 2009 concerning Social Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2009 Number 12, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4967);

12. Law Number 36 of 2009 concerning Health (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2009 Number 144, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5063);

13. Law Number 12 of 2011 concerning Formation of Legislation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5234);

14. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587), as amended several times most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);

15. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment Number 1 of 2008 concerning Guidelines for Improving the Quality of Life of Women;

16. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment Number 2 of 2008 concerning Guidelines for Implementation of Protection of Women; With Mutual Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF SOUTH BANGKA REGENCY and

REGENT OF SOUTH BANGKA

HAS DECIDED:

To Enact : REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING WOMEN'S EMPOWERMENT AND PROTECTION.

Article 16

Implementation of women's protection is provided to: a. female workers / laborers;

b. elderly women;

c. women with disabilities;

d. homeless women;

e. domestic workers;

f. women as family economic breadwinners;

g. female ex-inmates of correctional institutions; h. female disaster victims;

i. female commercial sex workers; and

j. female victims of violence.

Article 4

Women's Empowerment and Protection aims to: a. increase women's participation in the development process;

b. improve the quality of life of women;

c. provide guarantees to women in the fulfillment of their rights as human beings; and

d. provide security by increasing protection for women from various acts of violence.

Article 5

(1) Every woman has the right to:

a. live and sustain life and improve her standard of living;
b. fulfill her basic needs to grow and develop properly, have a family and continue her lineage;
c. develop her personality, obtain education, and improve her quality of life to become a faithful, devout, responsible, noble, happy, and prosperous human being in accordance with human rights;
d. obtain justice, security, and freedom to express opinions without discrimination;
e. be involved in every stage of the development process; f. be free from slavery or servitude and threats; g. obtain protection of her person, family, honor, dignity and property rights;
h. receive welfare and a decent life; i. participate in politics;
j. perform legal acts; and
k. freely choose her partner in marriage. (2) Every wife during marriage has the same rights and responsibilities as her husband in all matters relating to her marital life, relationship with her children, and ownership and management of joint property in accordance with applicable laws and regulations.
(3) After the dissolution of marriage, a woman has rights and responsibilities with her former husband in all matters relating to children with regard to the best interests of the child.

Article 20

(1) Implementation of protection for homeless women in order to provide protection and prevention against forms of violence caused by the absence of shelter or permanent residence.

(2) Homeless women as referred to in paragraph (1) receive the right to protection over:

a. decent housing;
b. health and social security;
c. population administration registration;
d. opportunities to obtain skills to improve economic status; and
e. access to information and legal consultation services.

Article 19

(1) Implementation of protection for women with disabilities in order to provide protection and prevention against forms of discrimination including freedom from cruel, inhumane treatment, degrading human dignity and freedom from exploitation;

(2) Women with disabilities as referred to in paragraph (1) receive the right to protection over: a. guarantee of continuity and personal self-development;

b. participation in the fields of education, health, employment, politics, sports, arts and culture as well as utilization of information and communication technology;

c. accessibility to information, legal assistance, mobility, social services; and

d. early intervention efforts including treatment and rehabilitation to improve their functions and capacities.

Article 2

The scope of this Regional Regulation includes:

a. Principles and objectives;
b. Women's rights;
c. Obligations and Responsibilities of Regional Government; d. Women's Empowerment;
e. Women's Protection;
f. Women's Empowerment and Protection Strategy; g. Mechanism for Implementation of Women's Empowerment and Protection;
h. Community Participation;
i. Funding; and
j. Guidance and Supervision.

Article 3

Women's Empowerment and Protection shall be implemented based on the principles of:

a. respect for human rights;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination; and
d. victim protection.

Article 32

(1) The Regent or OPD that has the field of women's empowerment conducts guidance and supervision over women's empowerment and protection;

(2) The forms of guidance as referred to in paragraph (1) are as follows:

a. providing socialization to the community and business community regarding policies on women's empowerment and protection;
b. providing books, leaflets, brochures on women's empowerment and protection, and other related issues and disseminating them to the community; c facilitation of capacity building for institutions implementing women's empowerment and protection;
d. facilitation of women's participation forums and women's organizations;
e. facilitation of rehabilitation and reintegration services for women victims of disasters and victims of violence;
f. coordinating the participation of the business community and other institutions, including international assistance for women's empowerment and protection; and
g. providing awards to the community and business community both individually and institutionally who have made efforts for women's empowerment and protection.
(3) The form of supervision as referred to in paragraph (1) may be in the form of monitoring and evaluation activities on women's empowerment and protection.

