1.10.text:
Article 19

(1) Implementation of protection for women with disabilities in order to provide protection and prevention against forms of discrimination including freedom from cruel, inhumane treatment, degrading human dignity and freedom from exploitation;

(2) Women with disabilities as referred to in paragraph (1) receive the right to protection over: a. guarantee of continuity and personal self-development;

b. participation in the fields of education, health, employment, politics, sports, arts and culture as well as utilization of information and communication technology;

c. accessibility to information, legal assistance, mobility, social services; and

d. early intervention efforts including treatment and rehabilitation to improve their functions and capacities.

Article 4

Women's Empowerment and Protection aims to: a. increase women's participation in the development process;

b. improve the quality of life of women;

c. provide guarantees to women in the fulfillment of their rights as human beings; and

d. provide security by increasing protection for women from various acts of violence.

Article 16

Implementation of women's protection is provided to: a. female workers / laborers;

b. elderly women;

c. women with disabilities;

d. homeless women;

e. domestic workers;

f. women as family economic breadwinners;

g. female ex-inmates of correctional institutions; h. female disaster victims;

i. female commercial sex workers; and

j. female victims of violence.

Article 3

Women's Empowerment and Protection shall be implemented based on the principles of:

a. respect for human rights;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination; and
d. victim protection.

Article 5

(1) Every woman has the right to:

a. live and sustain life and improve her standard of living;
b. fulfill her basic needs to grow and develop properly, have a family and continue her lineage;
c. develop her personality, obtain education, and improve her quality of life to become a faithful, devout, responsible, noble, happy, and prosperous human being in accordance with human rights;
d. obtain justice, security, and freedom to express opinions without discrimination;
e. be involved in every stage of the development process; f. be free from slavery or servitude and threats; g. obtain protection of her person, family, honor, dignity and property rights;
h. receive welfare and a decent life; i. participate in politics;
j. perform legal acts; and
k. freely choose her partner in marriage. (2) Every wife during marriage has the same rights and responsibilities as her husband in all matters relating to her marital life, relationship with her children, and ownership and management of joint property in accordance with applicable laws and regulations.
(3) After the dissolution of marriage, a woman has rights and responsibilities with her former husband in all matters relating to children with regard to the best interests of the child.

Article 2

The scope of this Regional Regulation includes:

a. Principles and objectives;
b. Women's rights;
c. Obligations and Responsibilities of Regional Government; d. Women's Empowerment;
e. Women's Protection;
f. Women's Empowerment and Protection Strategy; g. Mechanism for Implementation of Women's Empowerment and Protection;
h. Community Participation;
i. Funding; and
j. Guidance and Supervision.

2.1.text:
Article 32

(1) The Regent or OPD that has the field of women's empowerment conducts guidance and supervision over women's empowerment and protection;

(2) The forms of guidance as referred to in paragraph (1) are as follows:

a. providing socialization to the community and business community regarding policies on women's empowerment and protection;
b. providing books, leaflets, brochures on women's empowerment and protection, and other related issues and disseminating them to the community; c facilitation of capacity building for institutions implementing women's empowerment and protection;
d. facilitation of women's participation forums and women's organizations;
e. facilitation of rehabilitation and reintegration services for women victims of disasters and victims of violence;
f. coordinating the participation of the business community and other institutions, including international assistance for women's empowerment and protection; and
g. providing awards to the community and business community both individually and institutionally who have made efforts for women's empowerment and protection.
(3) The form of supervision as referred to in paragraph (1) may be in the form of monitoring and evaluation activities on women's empowerment and protection.

